R["«EXEC»"] = """*(Synthetic orders with hidden processing and shipping times, built on the documented design of Myntra's promise-date work: promise = warehouse time + shipping time, quantile regression for breach control, sale-day (HRD) models, and a rule-based baseline. Myntra's data and results are not reproduced and public method detail is thin; this is an interview-standard solution on simulated data. Written after the analysis.)*

- **The question:** which delivery date should checkout show so that late deliveries (breaches) stay at or below **5%**, with as little padding as possible, including on sale days?
- **A learned 95th-percentile model is far tighter than the rule-based table, with fewer breaches.** On normal (BAU) test days the rule-based zone table promises **5.97 days on average and is accurate (on the promised date or one day early) for only 16.6% of orders**, with 2.0% breach. Quantile regression promises 5.29 days with **1.3% breach** and 37.4% accuracy; its pinball loss is the lowest of the four candidates compared (0.149 vs 0.195 for the table, day-bootstrap CI of the gap 0.040 to 0.058).
- **But plain quantile regression is too cautious: 1.3% breach against a 5% target.** Promises are rounded up to whole days, which adds slack. **Split-conformal calibration** fixes this: BAU breach **4.9%** (day-bootstrap CI 3.8% to 5.9%, so we cannot reject 5%) with accuracy **73.7%** and a mean promise of 4.28 days.
- **The conformal guarantee behaves as theory says on exchangeable data.** Over 400 random calibration sets, mean coverage was 94.9% to 95.0% for n = 100, 500 and 2,000, matching the Beta law (KS p between 0.34 and 0.69).
- **It does not hold on sale days.** The same offset gives **9.6% breach on sale days and 11.8% after the sale** (coverage 90.4% and 88.2%). Mondrian per-zone calibration (BAU 4.9%, sale 10.1%) and 10-day rolling conformal (BAU 6.9%) do not repair this, because recent feedback is mostly unavailable: only **6.6%** of the last three days of orders have been delivered. A 3-day feedback correction is far worse (34% breach).
- **Segments are not equal under one offset.** Split-conformal breach on BAU days runs from 3.9% (remote) to 5.3% (tier-2) by zone, and is 6.0% for orders after the 16:00 cut-off versus 3.9% before; zone and cut-off differences remain significant after Holm correction (p = 0.008 and below 1e-4).
- **Recommended rule (added after seeing the result, so it needs a live check):** use the conformal offset on normal days and plain quantile regression on sale and post-sale days. On the test days this gives **4.9% BAU breach, 4.6% on sale days and 4.3% after**, against 6.8% and 6.4% for the rule-based table on sale and post-sale days, with a mean promise 0.21 days shorter than the table on those days.
- **Money (assumed elasticities):** versus the rule-based table on BAU days, split-conformal is worth about **₹15.8 crore a year in the base case** (worst case ₹-1.4 crore, best ₹42.3 crore), because the promise is 1.69 days shorter. Plain quantile regression is worth ₹19.3 crore in the base case with a smaller downside (₹7.4 crore worst).
- **Verification needs volume:** certifying 5% within 1 percentage point needs about **1,825 orders (4,034 with day clustering)**; no lane reaches that in a week (median 14 orders per lane), so certify by zone, not by lane.
- **I recommend:** ship quantile regression with a conformal offset on normal days, plain quantile regression on sale days, report breach by zone and cut-off daily, and confirm in a live test on the next sale."""
R["«O5»"] = """we removed **400 duplicate orders**, **150 orders with no delivery outcome** and fixed **60 distance unit errors** (more than 5 times the lane median, divided by 100). The clean data has **141,824 orders over 60 days**, of which **31,280 (22.1%) are on sale days**. The median delivery time is 3 days; the range is 1 to 18 days. The target is whole days with a long right tail, which is why a high quantile, not the mean, is the right object."""
R["«O61»"] = """delivery time depends on zone, distance, courier and sale days, and has a long right tail. On BAU days the mean is **3.46 days (sd 1.31, 95th percentile 6)**; on sale days it is **4.06 (sd 1.57, 95th percentile 7)** and after the sale 3.99 (sd 1.45). D correlates 0.48 with distance, 0.37 with a vendor-procured item and 0.22 with 3PL use. A single static number per zone therefore pads many lanes and still fails on others, and a model for the 95th percentile has to be allowed to vary with these drivers and with the calendar."""
R["«O71»"] = """**Plain quantile regression has the lowest BAU breach among the unconformalised rules (1.3%, CI 1.1% to 1.4%) but it is over-cautious**: the target is 5%, so it wastes padding (mean promise 5.29 days, accuracy 37.4%). The rule-based table has 2.0% breach, 5.97 days and only **16.6%** accuracy. Mean + 1 day gives 1.9% breach and 36.4% accuracy. The expectile (asymmetric loss) model gives 2.3% breach and 59.8% accuracy, so asymmetric loss lands on a breach rate that nobody chose, which echoes the paper's remark that it cannot control breaches deterministically. **Split-conformal hits the target on BAU days (4.9%, accuracy 73.7%, mean promise 4.28 days)**, and Mondrian by zone is almost identical (4.9%, 75.4%). On sale days all fixed rules breach more: rule-based 6.8%, quantile regression 4.6%, split-conformal **9.6%**. After the sale: 6.4%, 4.3% and **11.8%**. The 3-day feedback rule collapses (BAU 34.4%) and the 10-day rolling rule overshoots (BAU 6.9%, sale 10.7%). The hybrid equals split-conformal on BAU and plain quantile regression on sale days (4.6% and 4.3%)."""
R["«O72»"] = """on BAU days the rule-based table, mean + 1 day, expectile and plain quantile regression all sit under 5% (day-bootstrap CI upper limits 2.3%, 2.2%, 2.8% and 1.5%), so on breach alone they are safe but loose. **Split-conformal (4.9%, CI 3.8% to 5.9%) and Mondrian (4.9%, CI 3.8% to 6.0%) cannot be distinguished from 5%** (exact p = 0.81 and 0.76; the exact test is optimistic because orders on a day are correlated, so we rely on the bootstrap CI). The 3-day feedback rule (34.4%) fails, and the rolling 10-day rule (6.9%, CI 3.8% to 10.9%) is not certifiable (exact Holm p below 1e-4). The estimated ICC of daily breach for plain quantile regression is only 0.0006, but with about 2,000 orders a day the design effect is 2.2, so the effective sample is less than half of the nominal one. With only 10 BAU test days, the day-level CIs are wide (about ±1 percentage point)."""
R["«O73»"] = """**Quantile regression has fewer breaches than every other unconformalised rule on the same orders**: 0.75 percentage points fewer than the rule-based table (CI 0.48 to 1.03; 98 orders breached only under quantile regression against 249 only under the table), 0.65 fewer than mean + 1 day and 1.04 fewer than the expectile model (all Holm p below 1e-4). **Accuracy is much higher than the table** (+20.8 points, CI 11.1 to 29.3). Against mean + 1 day the accuracy difference is +1.0 point with a day-bootstrap CI of -4.8 to +6.8, so the two are not distinguishable on accuracy at the day level, even though McNemar on orders gives p = 0.001 (the order-level test ignores day clustering). The expectile model is more accurate than quantile regression (quantile regression is 22.4 points lower) only because it sits at a less safe place on the frontier, with a higher breach rate (2.3% against 1.3%)."""
R["«O74»"] = """the finite-sample theory holds: for 100, 500 and 2,000 calibration orders the mean coverage over 400 random splits was **94.87%, 94.97% and 95.00%** against 95.05%, 95.01% and 95.00% by the Beta law; the spread matched (0.0233, 0.0094, 0.0051 against 0.0215, 0.0097, 0.0049) and the KS tests against the Beta law gave p = 0.34, 0.69 and 0.69. Small calibration sets are noisy: with n = 100, 47% of calibrations fall below 95% coverage (theory 44%). The offset transfers to normal test days (**coverage 95.1%**) but **not to the sale (90.4%) or the post-sale days (88.2%)**. Mean scores are nearly equal (-2.37 in calibration, -2.33 BAU, -2.31 HRD, -2.10 post-sale), so the failure is in the shape of the tail, which the KS test confirms (D = 0.026 BAU with p = 0.0004; 0.063 on sale days; 0.109 post-sale, p below 1e-20). With 41,000 test orders even a small shift is significant, but the sale-day shift is large enough to matter."""
R["«O75»"] = """under one conformal offset the marginal 5% hides differences. On BAU days split-conformal breach is **5.3% in tier-2, 4.3% in metro and 3.9% in remote**, 5.1% for own-fleet orders and 4.4% for 3PL, and **6.0% for orders placed after 16:00 against 3.9% before**. Zone differences stay significant after Holm adjustment (p = 0.008 for split-conformal, 0.002 for Mondrian), as do cut-off differences (p below 1e-4), while courier and vendor-item gaps are not significant (p = 0.44 and 1.0). Mondrian by zone does not remove the zone differences (tier-2 4.4%, metro 5.8%, remote 5.7%), because the other drivers still differ inside each zone. The big failures are by period: split-conformal reaches 9.6% on sale days and 11.8% after, and the rule-based table fails on vendor items (13.6%). Plain quantile regression has no segment above 4.6% but over-covers everywhere (BAU segments 0.9% to 2.0%). The hybrid has a worst segment of 6.0% (orders after 16:00) and no significant difference by period (p = 0.87)."""
R["«O76»"] = """a fixed offset breaks on the sale: daily breach for split-conformal averages 4.9% on normal days and 9.6% on sale days; the rolling 10-day rule averages 6.9% on BAU and 10.7% on sale days, so recent feedback does not rescue it. The reason is delayed outcomes: only **6.6% of the last-3-day window and 62.8% of the last-10-day window** has been delivered when the correction is computed, and those orders are the fast ones (coverage at offset 0 is 100% among delivered orders in the 3-day window against 97.5% in the whole window). The 3-day rule therefore sets very negative offsets (mean -1.91 days, down to -2.8) and breaches 34% of BAU orders. Daily breach of the fixed rules is strongly autocorrelated (0.73 to 0.76), meaning bad days come in runs; the hybrid, which resets to plain quantile regression on sale days, has 4.9% on normal days and 4.6% on sale days and an autocorrelation of 0.23."""
R["«O77»"] = """**On this test, the mean + buffer family reaches higher accuracy at 5% breach than quantile regression** (interpolated 84.0% against 81.1%; conformal 73.7% from only four points), so we cannot claim that quantile regression dominates a well-set buffer on the frontier. Its advantage is elsewhere: the pinball loss at 0.95 is lowest (0.149; mean + empirical residual buffer 0.158, expectile 0.177, rule-based table 0.195), and the gap to the next best is 0.0097 (day-bootstrap CI 0.004 to 0.012). The rule-based table sits far inside the frontier (16.6% accuracy at 2.0% breach). A caution: the quantile predictions **cross**: a higher τ gives a lower prediction for 10.7% (0.5 to 0.8), 11.7% (0.8 to 0.9), 11.9% (0.9 to 0.95) and 1.8% (0.95 to 0.99) of test orders, because each τ is fitted separately and the target is whole days. Rounding to days makes the frontier a staircase, which is why accuracy-at-breach comparisons are noisy."""
R["«O78»"] = """the 95th percentile of delivery days rises by **2.70 days for a vendor-procured item** (CI 2.60 to 2.80), 1.01 days per 1,000 km, 0.96 days for remote zones (CI 0.85 to 1.08), **1.36 days on sale days** (CI 1.28 to 1.45), 0.92 days after the sale, 0.75 days for orders after the 16:00 cut-off and 0.61 days for 3PL shipments; item count (multi) has no effect (p = 0.42). Several effects are bigger at the 95th percentile than at the median (vendor item 2.70 vs 1.59, sale days 1.36 vs 0.37, 3PL 0.61 vs 0.30), which is the case for a quantile model: the tail has different drivers than the middle. Yesterday's backlog readings matter little (centre +0.08 days; warehouse -0.17 days, a sign that is not operationally sensible and probably reflects the noisy reading). Pseudo R-squared at τ = 0.95 is 0.30. This is a simple linear model for interpretation; the gradient boosting model is the one used for promises."""
R["«O79»"] = """to estimate a breach rate near 5% within ±1 percentage point we need **1,825 delivered orders**, or about **4,034** once daily clustering (design effect 2.2) is included; detecting a rise from 5% to 7% at 80% power needs 822 independent orders. In a normal week of test data there are 354 active lanes with a median of 14 orders each, **no lane has 1,825 orders** and only 20 have 200 or more. Zones are fine (2,932 metro, 8,655 tier-2 and 2,512 remote orders in the week). So breach should be certified by zone and by courier, and lane-level promises should borrow strength from the model rather than from lane-level calibration."""
R["«O8»"] = """the model leans first on **whether the order has a vendor-procured item (permutation importance 0.066)** and **distance (0.059)**, then planned sale volume (0.018), the last-mile centre (0.014) and the hour of order (0.013). That matches the main sources of delay in the simulation and the linear model in 7.8. The previous-day backlog readings are not among the top five, partly because they are noisy. Operationally, vendor-procured items and sale-day volume are where the safe date moves most, so they are where a pendency alert is most valuable."""
R["«O9»"] = """versus the rule-based table on BAU days, **split-conformal shortens the promise by 1.69 days but raises the breach rate** from 2.0% to 4.9%. The net value is about **₹15.8 crore a year in the base case**, ₹-1.4 crore in the worst case and **₹42.3 crore** in the best case. Plain quantile regression shortens the promise by 0.68 days with 0.75 points fewer breaches and is worth ₹19.3 crore (worst ₹7.4 crore, best ₹35.7 crore), the better risk-adjusted choice on BAU days because it has the smaller downside; mean + 1 day is worth ₹19.6 crore (₹6.1 crore worst). Mondrian is worth ₹16.5 crore and rolling conformal about ₹0.2 crore (worst case ₹-10.6 crore). The newsvendor quantile is **τ* = 0.97 in the base case (0.985 worst, 0.96 best)**, above the 95% the business chose, meaning that if breaches cost what we assume, the 5% limit is not tight enough and the plain quantile model is not too cautious. On sale and post-sale days the hybrid promises 5.76 days against 5.97 for the table, with 4.5% breach against 6.7% and accuracy (0 to -2) of 73.5% against 51.3%; split-conformal would shorten the promise to 4.76 days but breach 10.2% of those orders. All rupee figures depend on assumed elasticities that have no documented source."""
R["«O10»"] = """**Recommendation**

1. **Replace the rule-based table with a gradient-boosted 95th-percentile model** for the promised date, retrained daily on delivered orders.
2. **Calibrate with a split-conformal offset on normal days** (about -1.0 days on the rounded score scale in this data). It reaches 4.9% breach with 73.7% accuracy, against 2.0% and 16.6% for the table. If the business values breaches at the assumed ₹94 each, keep plain quantile regression (1.3% breach) instead.
3. **On sale and post-sale days use plain quantile regression with no offset** (4.6% and 4.3% breach in the test). A fixed conformal offset breaches 9.6% to 11.8% there.
4. **Do not use short-window feedback correction** while only a small share of recent orders is delivered (6.6% in a 3-day window).
5. **Monitor breach daily by zone and by cut-off timing**, with an alert at 6%, and certify by zone, not by lane (about 1,825 orders needed for ±1 point).
6. **Add an A/B test of the promise rule** to measure the real conversion effect of a shorter promise.

**Decision rule:** ship if BAU breach is at most 5% (day-level CI upper limit at most 6%), sale-day breach is at most 8%, and accuracy (0 to -1) is above the table's. Roll back a zone if its weekly breach exceeds 7%.

**Not yet known:** real conversion impact of a shorter promise, behaviour on real lanes and 3PL stalls, and whether the hybrid holds on a second sale event (it was chosen after seeing this one)."""
